SPURS: a workload's ready count is not spent on admission; SPURS_IDLE_PROBE=0 turns the idle probes off.

Resistance 3's WWS job manager asserts "IsListFinished()" (joblist.cpp 91 and 297, jobarraycontainer.cpp 105), 10-27
times a run: before it resets a job list it requires the list's workload to report readyCount 0 and contention 0
(cellSpursGetWorkloadInfo). The PPU asks for the "no name" list's workload with cellSpursReadyCountStore(wid, 1)
and its policy module stores the count back to 0 once the list is drained. The kernel spent the count on the first
admission, so the jobs added after that ran only when an idle probe happened to admit the workload: in a 170 s run
to Haven the probes found work 117763 times, the count 8480. The probes of the two SharedList workloads never found
work, and a check that caught any probe in progress (contention 1) asserted.

The ready count now stays as the guest wrote it: the workload is admitted while the count exceeds its contention
(the SPU count it asks for, as the Cell SDK describes cellSpursReadyCountStore). Signal bits and the workload flag are
still consumed on admission. SPURS_IDLE_PROBE=0 skips the probes (default on, as before); the host sets it.
With both: no assert in two 230 s runs, Haven loads and plays (with the count kept but no probes before this, the
level load hung on the "HAVEN, OK" card; without the count kept, the probes are what fed the list).

Test: tools/test_spurs_ready_count.py (new, manufactured policy modules on the real scheduler, probes off): a
workload with no count is not admitted; one store of 1 serves three admissions until the module clears it, then
nothing runs it (fails before: one admission); a count of 1 admits one SPU of two, 2 the second.
test_spurs_scheduler and test_spurs_coordinator_kick pass unchanged.

Upstream: not proposed yet (upstream's kernel consumes the count on purpose, dbff6e08; its comment cites another
title that kicks with inlined atomics).
